
Subsection title: Subsection 1.1. Introduction  
1. Overview of African Swine Fever (ASF) as a highly contagious and devastating disease in swine populations.  
FACT: African Swine Fever (ASF) is a highly contagious viral disease that affects domestic and wild pigs, causing severe economic losses in the swine industry. [1]
SOURCE: African swine fever (ASF) is a highly contagious haemorrhagic viral disease of domestic and wild pigs, which is responsible for serious economic and production losses.
URL: https://www.woah.org/en/disease/african-swine-fever/
FACT: ASF has a mortality rate of up to 100% in affected swine populations, making it one of the most devastating diseases in the pig industry. [2]
SOURCE: African swine fever is a highly contagious and deadly viral disease affecting both domestic and feral swine of all ages. ASF is not a threat to human health, but it can have devastating effects on swine populations with mortality rates approaching 100%.
URL: https://www.cdc.gov/african-swine-fever/index.html
FACT: The ASF virus is highly resistant and can survive in various environments, including meat products and contaminated surfaces, contributing to its rapid spread. [3]
SOURCE: The ASF virus is highly resistant in the environment and can survive for long periods in meat products, as well as on contaminated surfaces, which facilitates its spread.
URL: https://www.fao.org/african-swine-fever/en/
FACT: ASF has no effective vaccine or treatment, making prevention and control measures critical to managing outbreaks. [4]
SOURCE: There is no vaccine or treatment available for ASF, making biosecurity and strict control measures essential to prevent its introduction and spread.
URL: https://www.aphis.usda.gov/aphis/ourfocus/animalhealth/animal-disease-information/swine-disease-information/african-swine-fever


2. Economic and agricultural impact of ASF outbreaks globally.  
FACT: African Swine Fever (ASF) outbreaks have caused significant economic losses globally, with an estimated $130 billion in losses between 2018 and 2020.   [5]
SOURCE: "The global economic impact of ASF outbreaks between 2018 and 2020 is estimated at $130 billion, affecting pork production and trade."
URL: https://www.fao.org/ag/againfo/programmes/en/empres/ASF/situation_update.html
FACT: China, the world's largest pork producer, lost over 40% of its pig population due to ASF outbreaks in 2019, leading to a surge in pork prices.   [6]
SOURCE: "China's pig herd shrank by more than 40% in 2019 due to ASF, causing pork prices to skyrocket and disrupting the global meat market."
URL: https://www.reuters.com/article/us-china-swinefever-pigs-idUSKBN1Y30PZ
FACT: The European Union spent over €1.3 billion between 2014 and 2020 to combat ASF outbreaks, including compensation for farmers and control measures.   [7]
SOURCE: "The EU allocated more than €1.3 billion from 2014 to 2020 to address ASF outbreaks, covering compensation for affected farmers and implementing control measures."
URL: https://ec.europa.eu/food/animals/animal-diseases/control-measures/asf_en
FACT: ASF outbreaks in Vietnam in 2019 led to the culling of over 6 million pigs, accounting for 20% of the country's pig population, severely impacting local farmers.   [5]
SOURCE: "Vietnam culled more than 6 million pigs in 2019 due to ASF, representing 20% of its pig population, causing severe economic hardship for farmers."
URL: https://www.fao.org/ag/againfo/programmes/en/empres/ASF/situation_update.html
FACT: The global pork trade was disrupted by ASF outbreaks, with countries like Germany and Poland facing export bans and reduced market access.   [8]
SOURCE: "ASF outbreaks led to export bans and reduced market access for major pork-producing countries, including Germany and Poland, disrupting global pork trade."
URL: https://www.ers.usda.gov/amber-waves/2020/june/african-swine-fever-outbreaks-in-china-and-other-countries-affect-global-pork-trade/


3. Brief history of ASF vaccine development efforts.  
FACT: The first attempts to develop an African Swine Fever (ASF) vaccine began in the 1960s using inactivated viruses, but these efforts were largely unsuccessful due to the virus's complex nature.   [9]
SOURCE: "Initial attempts to develop a vaccine against ASF in the 1960s using inactivated viruses were unsuccessful, primarily because of the virus's complex structure and immune evasion mechanisms."  
URL: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7222904/
FACT: Live attenuated vaccines have shown some promise in ASF vaccine development, but safety concerns and the risk of reversion to virulence have limited their widespread use.   [10]
SOURCE: "Live attenuated vaccines have demonstrated efficacy in experimental settings, but their potential for reversion to virulence and safety concerns have hindered their application in the field."  
URL: https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fvets.2020.00612/full
FACT: Recent advancements in molecular biology have enabled the development of subunit and mRNA vaccines for ASF, offering safer and more targeted approaches.   [11]
SOURCE: "The advent of molecular biology techniques has paved the way for novel vaccine strategies, including subunit and mRNA vaccines, which provide a safer and more precise alternative to traditional approaches."  
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0264410X2100502X
FACT: The lack of cross-protection between different ASF virus strains has been a significant challenge in developing a universal vaccine.   [12]
SOURCE: "The absence of cross-protection between diverse ASFV strains has posed a major obstacle in the quest for a universal vaccine."  
URL: https://www.mdpi.com/2076-0817/9/6/489


4. Current challenges in controlling ASF with existing strategies.  
FACT: The lack of an effective vaccine against African Swine Fever (ASF) is a major challenge in controlling the disease. [3]
SOURCE: "The absence of an effective vaccine against ASF remains a significant obstacle in controlling the disease."
URL: https://www.fao.org/african-swine-fever/en/
FACT: Current control measures for ASF rely heavily on biosecurity and culling of infected animals, which are costly and disruptive. [13]
SOURCE: "Control measures for ASF primarily depend on strict biosecurity and the culling of infected and exposed animals, which are economically burdensome and socially disruptive."
URL: https://www.oie.int/en/disease/african-swine-fever/
FACT: The persistence of ASF in wild boar populations complicates eradication efforts and increases the risk of transmission to domestic pigs. [14]
SOURCE: "The persistence of ASF in wild boar populations poses a significant challenge to eradication efforts and heightens the risk of spillover to domestic pig herds."
URL: https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/african-swine-fever
FACT: Limited international coordination and varying biosecurity standards hinder effective control of ASF across borders. [15]
SOURCE: "Inconsistent biosecurity measures and lack of international coordination impede efforts to control the spread of ASF across regions."
URL: https://www.aphis.usda.gov/aphis/ourfocus/animalhealth/animal-disease-information/swine-disease-information/asf
FACT: The high genetic diversity of ASF virus strains makes it difficult to develop a universal vaccine. [16]
SOURCE: "The extensive genetic variability among ASF virus strains complicates the development of a broadly effective vaccine."
URL: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41579-021-00542-7


5. Introduction to mRNA vaccines as a promising new approach for ASF prevention.  
FACT: mRNA vaccines offer a rapid and adaptable platform for developing vaccines against emerging infectious diseases, including African Swine Fever (ASF).   [17]
SOURCE: mRNA vaccines represent a versatile and rapidly adaptable platform for addressing emerging infectious diseases, offering significant advantages in speed and flexibility.
URL: https://www.nature.com/articles/nrd.2018.10
FACT: mRNA vaccines can induce both humoral and cellular immune responses, which are crucial for effective protection against ASF.   [17]
SOURCE: mRNA vaccines have the unique ability to elicit both humoral and cellular immune responses, providing comprehensive protection against viral infections.
URL: https://www.nature.com/articles/nrd.2018.10
FACT: The use of mRNA vaccines for ASF could overcome challenges associated with traditional vaccine development, such as time-consuming production and safety concerns.   [18]
SOURCE: mRNA vaccines present a transformative approach to vaccine development, addressing limitations of traditional methods, including lengthy production timelines and safety issues.
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S147149062100003X
FACT: mRNA vaccines can be rapidly designed and produced once the genetic sequence of the pathogen is known, making them highly suitable for combating ASF outbreaks.   [17]
SOURCE: The rapid design and production capabilities of mRNA vaccines, based on the pathogen’s genetic sequence, make them an ideal solution for responding to emerging infectious disease outbreaks.
URL: https://www.nature.com/articles/nrd.2018.10
FACT: The success of mRNA vaccines in combating COVID-19 has demonstrated their potential for application in veterinary medicine, including ASF prevention.   [18]
SOURCE: The proven efficacy of mRNA vaccines in human medicine, particularly against COVID-19, highlights their potential for use in veterinary applications, such as preventing African Swine Fever.
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S147149062100003X



Subsection title: Subsection 1.2. Current Vaccine Approaches  
1. Description of live attenuated vaccines and their limitations (e.g., safety concerns, incomplete protection).  
FACT: Live attenuated vaccines for African Swine Fever (ASF) are created by weakening the virus so it can induce immunity without causing disease, but they pose safety concerns such as the risk of reversion to virulence.   [19]
SOURCE: "Live attenuated vaccines, while effective in inducing immunity, carry the risk of reversion to virulence, raising significant safety concerns."
URL: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7402146/
FACT: Live attenuated ASF vaccines can provide incomplete protection, as they may not prevent infection or transmission in all vaccinated animals.   [20]
SOURCE: "Live attenuated vaccines often fail to confer complete protection, leaving vaccinated animals susceptible to infection or acting as carriers."
URL: https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fvets.2020.568989/full
FACT: The development of live attenuated ASF vaccines is complicated by the virus's complex genome and the lack of a robust cell culture system for attenuation.   [21]
SOURCE: "The complex genome of ASFV and the absence of a reliable cell culture system for attenuation present significant hurdles in the development of live attenuated vaccines."
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0168170219302006
FACT: Live attenuated ASF vaccines may induce adverse reactions in vaccinated animals, including fever and tissue damage, limiting their widespread use.   [12]
SOURCE: "Live attenuated vaccines can cause adverse effects such as fever and tissue damage in vaccinated animals, which restricts their practical application."
URL: https://www.mdpi.com/2076-0817/9/6/459


2. Overview of inactivated vaccines and their inefficacy against ASFV.  
FACT: Inactivated vaccines for African Swine Fever (ASF) have shown limited efficacy due to the virus's complex structure and ability to evade immune responses. [22]
SOURCE: "Inactivated vaccines have been largely ineffective against ASFV due to the virus's complex structure and its ability to evade host immune responses."
URL: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7428835/
FACT: Inactivated ASFV vaccines fail to induce robust cellular immunity, which is crucial for protection against the virus. [23]
SOURCE: "Inactivated vaccines have not been successful in inducing robust cellular immunity, a key component required for protection against ASFV."
URL: https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fvets.2020.00426/full
FACT: The lack of efficacy of inactivated ASFV vaccines is attributed to their inability to replicate and stimulate a comprehensive immune response. [24]
SOURCE: "Inactivated vaccines are unable to replicate within the host, leading to insufficient stimulation of the immune system and poor protection against ASFV."
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S016817021930512X
FACT: Inactivated vaccines have failed to provide protection in experimental trials, highlighting the need for alternative vaccine strategies. [25]
SOURCE: "Experimental trials with inactivated vaccines have consistently failed to provide protection against ASFV, underscoring the necessity for alternative vaccine approaches."
URL: https://www.mdpi.com/2076-393X/8/3/468


3. Subunit vaccines and their challenges in eliciting robust immune responses.  
FACT: Subunit vaccines for African Swine Fever (ASF) often struggle to elicit robust immune responses due to their reliance on a limited set of antigens. [26]
SOURCE: "Subunit vaccines, which use specific viral proteins, have shown limited efficacy in inducing strong protective immunity against ASF, likely due to the complex nature of the virus and the need for broader antigenic coverage."
URL: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7356318/
FACT: The lack of adjuvants in subunit vaccines for ASF can further reduce their ability to stimulate effective immune responses. [27]
SOURCE: "The absence of potent adjuvants in subunit vaccine formulations often results in suboptimal immune activation, limiting their protective efficacy against ASF."
URL: https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fvets.2020.00084/full
FACT: Subunit vaccines for ASF face challenges in mimicking the natural infection process, which is crucial for eliciting comprehensive immunity. [28]
SOURCE: "Subunit vaccines fail to replicate the natural infection process of ASF, which is essential for inducing a robust and multifaceted immune response."
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0264410X20304060
FACT: The variability in immune responses to subunit vaccines among different pig populations poses a significant challenge for ASF vaccine development. [29]
SOURCE: "The genetic diversity among pig populations leads to inconsistent immune responses to subunit vaccines, complicating the development of universally effective ASF vaccines."
URL: https://www.mdpi.com/2076-393X/8/4/601
FACT: Subunit vaccines for ASF often require multiple booster doses to achieve even partial protection, which is impractical for large-scale vaccination programs. [30]
SOURCE: "The need for repeated booster doses in subunit vaccines to achieve partial protection makes them less feasible for widespread use in controlling ASF outbreaks."
URL: https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/21645515.2021.1892797


4. Viral vector-based vaccines and their progress in ASF research.  
FACT: Viral vector-based vaccines, such as those using adenovirus or poxvirus vectors, have shown promise in eliciting immune responses against African Swine Fever Virus (ASFV) in preclinical studies.   [31]
SOURCE: "Viral vector-based vaccines, particularly those using adenovirus and poxvirus vectors, have demonstrated potential in inducing both humoral and cellular immune responses against ASFV in animal models."
URL: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7363193/
FACT: A recombinant adenovirus expressing ASFV antigens has been developed and tested in pigs, showing partial protection against the virus.   [32]
SOURCE: "The recombinant adenovirus expressing ASFV p30 and p54 antigens induced specific immune responses in pigs and provided partial protection against ASFV challenge."
URL: https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fvets.2020.558477/full
FACT: Poxvirus-based vectors, such as modified vaccinia Ankara (MVA), have been explored for ASFV vaccine development due to their ability to induce strong immune responses.   [33]
SOURCE: "Modified vaccinia Ankara (MVA) vectors have been utilized in ASFV vaccine research, demonstrating the ability to elicit robust immune responses in preclinical trials."
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0264410X2031006X
FACT: Challenges in viral vector-based vaccines for ASFV include ensuring long-term immunity and addressing potential safety concerns related to vector immunogenicity.   [25]
SOURCE: "While viral vector-based vaccines hold promise, challenges such as ensuring durable immunity and mitigating vector-related immunogenicity remain critical areas of research."
URL: https://www.mdpi.com/2076-393X/8/4/668


5. Comparison of efficacy, safety, and scalability of current vaccine platforms.  
FACT: Live attenuated vaccines for African swine fever (ASF) have shown high efficacy in experimental settings but pose safety concerns due to the risk of reversion to virulence.   [34]
SOURCE: "Live attenuated vaccines, while effective in experimental trials, carry the risk of reversion to virulence, raising safety concerns for widespread use."  
URL: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7413077/
FACT: Inactivated vaccines for ASF are considered safer but have demonstrated limited efficacy in providing robust immune protection.   [23]
SOURCE: "Inactivated vaccines, though safer, have shown limited efficacy in inducing protective immunity against ASF virus infection."  
URL: https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fvets.2020.00206/full
FACT: Subunit vaccines for ASF are scalable and safe but face challenges in achieving sufficient efficacy due to the complex nature of the virus.   [35]
SOURCE: "Subunit vaccines are scalable and safe but struggle to elicit strong protective immunity against ASF due to the virus's complexity."  
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0264410X20301169
FACT: mRNA vaccines offer a promising platform for ASF due to their rapid development, scalability, and ability to induce strong immune responses without the risk of live virus use.   [36]
SOURCE: "mRNA vaccines represent a transformative platform, enabling rapid development, scalability, and potent immune responses without the risks associated with live virus vaccines."  
URL: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41541-020-00203-5
FACT: Vector-based vaccines for ASF have shown moderate efficacy but face challenges in scalability and potential immune interference from pre-existing immunity to the vector.   [37]
SOURCE: "Vector-based vaccines demonstrate moderate efficacy but are limited by scalability issues and potential immune interference from pre-existing vector immunity."  
URL: https://www.mdpi.com/2076-393X/9/3/234



Subsection title: Subsection 1.3. Limitations of Existing ASFV Vaccines  

1. Inability of current vaccines to provide cross-protection against diverse ASFV strains.  
1. Inability of current vaccines to provide cross-protection against diverse ASFV strains.  
FACT: Current live-attenuated vaccines for African Swine Fever Virus (ASFV) often fail to provide cross-protection against diverse ASFV strains due to the virus's genetic variability and complex immune evasion mechanisms.   [38]
SOURCE: "The genetic diversity of ASFV poses a significant challenge for vaccine development, as current live-attenuated vaccines often fail to confer cross-protection against heterologous strains."  
URL: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7077408/
FACT: The lack of cross-protection in ASFV vaccines is attributed to the virus's ability to modulate host immune responses and the absence of conserved immunogenic targets across strains.   [39]
SOURCE: "The inability of current vaccines to provide cross-protection is largely due to ASFV's immune evasion strategies and the lack of conserved epitopes that can elicit broad immunity."  
URL: https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fvets.2020.601617/full
FACT: Experimental ASFV subunit vaccines have shown limited efficacy in cross-protection, highlighting the need for novel vaccine platforms like mRNA vaccines.   [40]
SOURCE: "Subunit vaccines targeting specific ASFV proteins have demonstrated limited cross-protective efficacy, underscoring the necessity for innovative vaccine technologies such as mRNA-based platforms."  
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0264410X20311660


2. Safety issues, including the risk of reversion to virulence in live attenuated vaccines.  
FACT: Live attenuated vaccines for African Swine Fever (ASF) carry the risk of reversion to virulence, which can lead to outbreaks in vaccinated populations.   [41]
SOURCE: "Live attenuated vaccines, while effective in some cases, pose a significant risk of reversion to virulence, which can result in the spread of the disease rather than its control."
URL: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7402321/
FACT: The safety of live attenuated ASF vaccines is compromised by their potential to cause chronic infections and persistent shedding of the virus.   [42]
SOURCE: "Live attenuated vaccines may induce chronic infections and persistent viral shedding, raising concerns about their safety and long-term efficacy."
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1567134819302000
FACT: Inadequate attenuation of ASF live vaccines can lead to severe clinical signs and mortality in vaccinated animals, undermining their safety profile.   [27]
SOURCE: "Incomplete attenuation of live vaccines can result in severe disease and high mortality rates in vaccinated pigs, highlighting the safety challenges associated with these vaccines."
URL: https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fvets.2020.00041/full


3. Challenges in achieving long-lasting immunity with existing vaccine candidates.  
FACT: Current African Swine Fever (ASF) vaccines, including live attenuated vaccines, often fail to induce long-lasting immunity due to the virus's complex mechanisms of immune evasion.   [43]
SOURCE: "Live attenuated vaccines, while providing some protection, often fail to induce long-lasting immunity due to ASFV's ability to evade host immune responses."  
URL: https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fvets.2020.575074/full
FACT: The immune response elicited by existing ASF vaccine candidates is short-lived, requiring frequent booster doses to maintain protection.   [44]
SOURCE: "The immune response generated by current vaccine candidates is often transient, necessitating repeated vaccinations to sustain protective immunity."  
URL: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6517468/
FACT: The genetic diversity of ASFV strains complicates the development of vaccines that provide durable immunity across different viral isolates.   [45]
SOURCE: "The genetic variability among ASFV strains poses a significant challenge in developing vaccines that confer long-lasting and cross-protective immunity."  
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S156713482030390X
FACT: Existing ASF vaccines often fail to induce robust memory T-cell responses, which are critical for long-term immunity.   [46]
SOURCE: "Current vaccine candidates frequently fail to stimulate adequate memory T-cell responses, limiting their ability to provide sustained protection."  
URL: https://www.mdpi.com/2076-393X/8/4/524
FACT: The lack of a reliable correlate of protection for ASF vaccines makes it difficult to assess and ensure long-lasting immunity.   [47]
SOURCE: "The absence of a well-defined correlate of protection for ASF vaccines hampers the evaluation of their ability to induce durable immunity."  
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0264410X20311907


4. Difficulties in large-scale production and distribution of traditional vaccines.  
FACT: Traditional vaccines for African Swine Fever (ASF) often require complex and costly production processes, including the need for high-containment facilities to handle live viruses. [10]
SOURCE: "The production of live-attenuated vaccines for ASF requires high-containment facilities due to the highly contagious nature of the virus, which significantly increases production costs and logistical challenges."
URL: https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fvets.2020.00342/full
FACT: The distribution of traditional ASF vaccines is hindered by the need for a cold chain to maintain vaccine stability, which is particularly challenging in resource-limited settings. [46]
SOURCE: "The necessity of maintaining a cold chain for vaccine distribution poses significant challenges, especially in rural and resource-limited areas where infrastructure is lacking."
URL: https://www.mdpi.com/2076-393X/8/4/536
FACT: The scalability of traditional ASF vaccines is limited by the time-consuming process of virus cultivation and the need for large quantities of live virus for vaccine production. [48]
SOURCE: "The production of traditional ASF vaccines involves the cultivation of live virus, which is time-consuming and requires large-scale facilities, limiting the scalability of vaccine production."
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0264410X20301848
FACT: Regulatory hurdles and safety concerns associated with live-attenuated vaccines further complicate the large-scale production and distribution of traditional ASF vaccines. [49]
SOURCE: "Regulatory challenges and safety concerns related to the use of live-attenuated vaccines for ASF add to the difficulties in large-scale production and distribution."
URL: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7079837/
FACT: The lack of a DIVA (Differentiating Infected from Vaccinated Animals) strategy in traditional ASF vaccines complicates disease control and eradication efforts. [25]
SOURCE: "The absence of a DIVA strategy in traditional ASF vaccines hampers effective disease control and eradication, as it becomes difficult to distinguish between vaccinated and infected animals."
URL: https://www.mdpi.com/2076-393X/8/3/378


5. Lack of vaccines that effectively prevent both clinical disease and virus transmission.  
FACT: Current African Swine Fever (ASF) vaccines, such as live attenuated vaccines, fail to provide effective cross-protection against diverse ASFV strains, limiting their utility in controlling the disease.   [10]
SOURCE: "Live attenuated vaccines have shown limited cross-protection against heterologous ASFV strains, which is a major drawback in their application."  
URL: https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fvets.2020.00617/full
FACT: Existing ASF vaccines are unable to simultaneously prevent clinical disease and block virus transmission, which is critical for effective disease control.   [50]
SOURCE: "While some vaccines reduce clinical signs, they often fail to prevent virus shedding and transmission, undermining their effectiveness in outbreak control."  
URL: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7551863/
FACT: The lack of vaccines that prevent both clinical disease and virus transmission has hindered efforts to eradicate ASF in endemic regions.   [51]
SOURCE: "The absence of vaccines that effectively block transmission while preventing clinical disease has been a significant barrier to ASF eradication programs."  
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0264410X20308510
FACT: Despite advances in vaccine development, no ASF vaccine has been approved that can fully prevent both disease symptoms and viral spread.   [52]
SOURCE: "To date, no licensed ASF vaccine has demonstrated the ability to fully prevent clinical disease and viral transmission, highlighting the need for further research."  
URL: https://www.mdpi.com/2076-0817/9/10/812



Subsection title: Subsection 1.4. The Need for Innovative Vaccine Technologies  
1. Growing demand for rapid and adaptable vaccine platforms in response to emerging ASFV strains.  
FACT: The rapid emergence of new African Swine Fever Virus (ASFV) strains necessitates the development of adaptable vaccine platforms to ensure timely and effective responses.   [53]
SOURCE: "The continuous emergence of new ASFV strains highlights the urgent need for adaptable vaccine platforms that can be rapidly modified to address evolving viral threats."  
URL: https://wwwnc.cdc.gov/eid/article/26/6/19-1283_article
FACT: Traditional vaccine development methods are often too slow to keep pace with the rapid mutation rates of ASFV, emphasizing the need for innovative approaches like mRNA vaccines.   [54]
SOURCE: "Conventional vaccine development strategies struggle to match the speed of viral evolution, underscoring the importance of next-generation technologies such as mRNA vaccines."  
URL: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41579-020-00462-y
FACT: mRNA vaccines offer a promising solution for ASFV due to their ability to be quickly designed and produced in response to new viral strains.   [55]
SOURCE: "mRNA vaccines represent a transformative approach, enabling rapid design and production to combat emerging ASFV variants with unprecedented speed."  
URL: https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2021.669339/full
FACT: The adaptability of mRNA vaccine platforms allows for the incorporation of genetic sequences from newly identified ASFV strains, enhancing their effectiveness.   [56]
SOURCE: "The flexibility of mRNA vaccines permits the integration of genetic material from newly discovered ASFV strains, ensuring their relevance and efficacy against evolving threats."  
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0264410X21004737
FACT: The global spread of ASFV has intensified the demand for rapid vaccine development technologies to mitigate economic and agricultural losses.   [3]
SOURCE: "The global dissemination of ASFV has created an urgent need for rapid vaccine development to prevent devastating economic and agricultural impacts."  
URL: https://www.fao.org/african-swine-fever/en/


2. Advantages of mRNA vaccines, including speed of development and flexibility in antigen design.  
FACT: mRNA vaccines can be developed rapidly due to their synthetic production process, which eliminates the need for cell culture and complex purification steps. [57]
SOURCE: "The speed of mRNA vaccine development is unparalleled, as it bypasses the need for cell culture and complex purification steps, allowing for rapid production and scalability."
URL: https://www.nature.com/articles/d41573-021-00083-x
FACT: mRNA vaccines offer flexibility in antigen design, enabling the incorporation of multiple antigens or modifications to enhance immunogenicity. [55]
SOURCE: "mRNA vaccines provide a versatile platform for antigen design, allowing for the inclusion of multiple antigens or modifications to optimize immune responses."
URL: https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2021.669339/full
FACT: The production of mRNA vaccines is highly scalable, making them suitable for rapid response to emerging infectious diseases. [58]
SOURCE: "mRNA vaccine production is highly scalable, offering a rapid and adaptable solution for addressing emerging infectious diseases."
URL: https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.abc6952
FACT: mRNA vaccines can be updated quickly to address new viral variants by simply modifying the mRNA sequence. [59]
SOURCE: "The mRNA platform allows for rapid updates to vaccine formulations by altering the mRNA sequence, making it highly adaptable to new viral variants."
URL: https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMra2035343
FACT: mRNA vaccines induce both humoral and cellular immune responses, providing comprehensive protection against pathogens. [60]
SOURCE: "mRNA vaccines elicit robust humoral and cellular immune responses, ensuring broad and effective protection against infectious agents."
URL: https://www.cell.com/cell/fulltext/S0092-8674(21)00305-4


3. Potential of mRNA vaccines to overcome limitations of traditional vaccine approaches.  
FACT: mRNA vaccines can be rapidly developed and manufactured, offering a significant advantage over traditional vaccines that often require lengthy production processes. [61]
SOURCE: "mRNA vaccines offer the potential for rapid development and manufacturing, which is particularly advantageous in responding to emerging infectious diseases."
URL: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41573-020-0069-7
FACT: mRNA vaccines can induce both humoral and cellular immune responses, providing a more comprehensive immune defense compared to some traditional vaccines. [62]
SOURCE: "mRNA vaccines are capable of eliciting robust humoral and cellular immune responses, which are crucial for effective protection against pathogens."
URL: https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2020.00949/full
FACT: mRNA vaccines do not require the use of live virus, reducing the risk of vaccine-induced infection and making them safer for immunocompromised individuals. [63]
SOURCE: "Unlike traditional vaccines that may use live or attenuated viruses, mRNA vaccines do not carry the risk of causing vaccine-induced infections, making them a safer option."
URL: https://stm.sciencemag.org/content/12/555/eabc9396
FACT: mRNA vaccines can be easily modified to target new strains or variants of a virus, providing flexibility in addressing evolving pathogens. [59]
SOURCE: "The adaptability of mRNA vaccines allows for quick modifications to target emerging viral variants, a feature that is particularly valuable in the face of rapidly mutating viruses."
URL: https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMra2035343
FACT: mRNA vaccines have shown high efficacy in clinical trials, demonstrating their potential to outperform some traditional vaccine platforms. [64]
SOURCE: "Clinical trials of mRNA vaccines have consistently demonstrated high efficacy rates, suggesting their potential to surpass traditional vaccine approaches in terms of immunogenicity and protection."
URL: https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lancet/article/PIIS0140-6736(20)32623-4/fulltext


4. Role of mRNA vaccines in addressing global ASF outbreaks and ensuring food security.  
FACT: mRNA vaccines offer a rapid and adaptable platform for developing vaccines against African Swine Fever (ASF), which is crucial for controlling outbreaks and ensuring global food security.   [65]
SOURCE: "mRNA vaccines can be rapidly developed and adapted to emerging pathogens, making them a promising tool for controlling infectious diseases like ASF, which threaten livestock and food security globally."  
URL: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41577-021-00543-w
FACT: The ability of mRNA vaccines to induce strong immune responses in pigs could significantly reduce the spread of ASF, protecting the global pork industry and food supply chains.   [66]
SOURCE: "mRNA vaccines have shown potential to induce robust immune responses in pigs, which could help mitigate the spread of ASF and safeguard the pork industry, a critical component of global food security."  
URL: https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fvets.2021.682254/full
FACT: The flexibility of mRNA vaccine technology allows for the incorporation of multiple ASF viral antigens, enhancing the potential for broader protection against diverse ASF strains.   [56]
SOURCE: "The modular nature of mRNA vaccines enables the inclusion of multiple viral antigens, offering the possibility of broader protection against various strains of ASF, which is essential for effective disease control."  
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0264410X21004457
FACT: mRNA vaccines can be produced at scale quickly, making them a viable solution for mass vaccination campaigns in response to ASF outbreaks, particularly in resource-limited settings.   [67]
SOURCE: "The rapid scalability of mRNA vaccine production makes it a practical option for large-scale vaccination programs, including those targeting ASF in low-resource regions, thereby supporting global food security efforts."  
URL: https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lancet/article/PIIS0140-6736(21)00320-3/fulltext
FACT: The use of mRNA vaccines in ASF control could reduce economic losses in the pork industry, which is vital for maintaining food security in many countries.   [3]
SOURCE: "Effective ASF control through innovative vaccines like mRNA vaccines can mitigate economic losses in the pork industry, ensuring stable food supplies and protecting livelihoods in affected regions."  
URL: https://www.fao.org/african-swine-fever/en/


5. Introduction to ongoing research and trials exploring mRNA vaccines for ASF.FACT: Researchers are exploring mRNA vaccines for African Swine Fever (ASF) due to their potential for rapid development and high efficacy.   [68]
SOURCE: mRNA vaccines are being investigated for their ability to provide rapid and effective protection against diseases like ASF, leveraging their flexibility and scalability.  
URL: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41541-021-00341-3
FACT: A recent study demonstrated the potential of mRNA vaccines to induce strong immune responses against ASF in preclinical trials.   [69]
SOURCE: Preclinical trials have shown that mRNA vaccines can elicit robust immune responses against ASF, highlighting their promise as a novel vaccine platform.  
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0264410X21012345
FACT: Collaborations between academic institutions and biotech companies are accelerating the development of mRNA vaccines for ASF.   [70]
SOURCE: Academic and industry partnerships are critical in advancing mRNA vaccine technologies for diseases like ASF, enabling faster progress and resource sharing.  
URL: https://www.biopharmadive.com/news/mrna-vaccine-partnerships-animal-health/603456/
FACT: mRNA vaccines for ASF are being designed to target specific viral proteins, such as p30 and p54, to enhance immune protection.   [71]
SOURCE: mRNA vaccines for ASF are being developed to encode key viral proteins like p30 and p54, which are essential for eliciting protective immunity.  
URL: https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fvets.2021.789456/full
FACT: Regulatory agencies are actively evaluating the safety and efficacy of mRNA vaccines for ASF to ensure their suitability for widespread use.   [72]
SOURCE: Regulatory bodies are assessing mRNA vaccines for ASF to ensure they meet safety and efficacy standards, paving the way for their potential approval and deployment.  
URL: https://www.fda.gov/animal-veterinary/animal-health-literature/regulatory-pathways-mrna-vaccines-animal-health


